// `avg` OVER A STRUCT-TYPED COLUMN IS REFUSED AT THE FRONT END.
//
// ⚠ THIS FILE PREVIOUSLY PINNED A HOLE, AND ITS OWN HEADER SAID SO. Until
// 7f12908d it expected `cannot cast 'Inner' to 'f64'` — the HOST COMPILER,
// reporting from `<metaprog-blob-subst>:3939` inside a generated blob, naming
// neither the clause nor the aggregate nor the column. The program was refused
// and no object was written, so nothing unsound ever shipped; the cost was
// entirely the diagnostic. That header said the expectation WAS the defect and
// that closing the hole meant rewriting this file. This is that rewrite.
//
// THE HOLE. `el_ty_of_name` (el.logos) launders every unknown type NAME to
// `EL_TY_INT` — the default arm of a 4-valued lattice. So a struct-typed column
// reached the shared rule table spelled INT, `agg_result_ty("avg", INT)`
// answered FLT, `agg_cast` came out true, and `compute_agg_bodies` skips
// `check_agg_operand` exactly on `agg_cast`. The argument was never asked about.
//
// ⚠ AND THE HOLE WAS WIDER THAN THE `agg_cast` SKIP — TWO LAUNDERING SITES, ONE
// CLASS. `sum`/`min`/`max` are NOT skipped: they reach `check_agg_operand`, and
// it passed them anyway, because it asks `infer_emit_ty` and `infer_emit_ty`
// launders too (codegen.logos — an out-of-lattice NAME falls back to
// `el_class_repr(infer_ty(…))`, which is `i64`). Measured on this same struct:
// `sum` gave `call to 'el_add' arg 2: expected i64, got Inner`, `min`/`max` gave
// `let '__v': type mismatch — expected i64, got Inner`. Two mechanisms reaching
// one wrong outcome, which is why the fix is a check on the ARGUMENT'S NAME
// (`infer_ty_name`, the question that stays unresolvable) placed BEFORE both,
// and why `wql_agg_sum_struct_arg_fail` exists as its sibling: closing only the
// skip would have left the other half green and unnoticed.
//
// ⚠ WHAT WAS NOT WEAKENED. `check_agg_operand`'s own lenient `ty.cls < 0`
// early-return is untouched, and so is `el_ty_of_name`'s INT default — the
// documented lenient policy for arithmetic operands, where the host compiler is
// the stated oracle, still holds. The new refusal is narrow by construction: it
// fires only on an aggregate ARGUMENT, only when the argument does not compute
// in the EL's own representation (so `sum(s.a + s.b)` over two f32 columns still
// compiles and answers in f64 — the arithmetic seam does its half), and only
// when the query NAMED a type the lattice has no class for.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct Inner { pub a: i64 }
struct Rec { pub k: i64, pub v: Inner }

pub deem means(src: &[Rec]) { from src s
      group by s.k
      aggregate a = avg(s.v)
      select a : f64 }

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
